# DECIDED: no `globalDefault: false` on any of these, though it read that way until
# 2026-08-27. It is the default, so stating it changes nothing about scheduling — and stating
# it made every `kubectl apply` of this file report "configured" forever. The API server drops
# globalDefault when it is false (an omitempty zero value), so the field is present in the
# manifest and in kubectl's last-applied annotation but absent from the live object. The
# three-way merge therefore finds a patch to make on every single run, and never converges.
#
# Measured on daniel-stage: two consecutive applies of the old file both printed "configured"
# for all four classes; with the field removed, the first converges and the second prints
# "unchanged". That was 4 of the 5 tasks reporting a spurious change on a settled cluster.
# ENFORCED by ansible/tests/k8s/test_priorityclass_omits_defaulted_fields.py.
#
# The homelab's workload tiers, as PriorityClasses. Four bands, all below
# system-node-critical (2000001000) and system-cluster-critical (2000000000) on purpose:
# the scheduler, CNI and kubelet plumbing still win every contest against a workload.
#
# WHAT THESE ACTUALLY DO, so the next reader does not overestimate them. Priority decides
# two things and nothing else: which pod the scheduler preempts when a node has no room,
# and the order the kubelet evicts under node pressure. It does NOT throttle CPU, reserve
# memory, or influence normal placement. With requests sitting near 36% of both nodes, the
# scheduler currently never has to preempt — so in practice these are insurance against
# memory pressure on daniel-box, which runs at ~60% with limits overcommitted to 160%.
#
# QoS RANKS ABOVE PRIORITY AT EVICTION. The kubelet sorts by QoS class first (BestEffort,
# then Burstable, then Guaranteed) and only uses priority to break ties within a class.
# Essentially every workload here is Burstable (requests below limits), so the tiers do
# discriminate — but a Guaranteed tier-4 pod would still outlive a Burstable tier-1 one.
# Getting the tiers right is not a substitute for getting requests right.
#
# EVERY homelab workload should carry one of these. A pod with no priorityClassName sits at
# 0, which is also where unclassified infrastructure in kube-system, longhorn-system and
# observability sits — so leaving a tier-4 workload unclassed does not place it "below tier
# 3", it merges it into a pile of pods that were never part of this model at all. That is
# why homelab-best-effort exists as a real class rather than as the absence of one.
---
# TIER 1 — base infrastructure: all ingress, all auth, LAN DNS, the WAF, the image registry,
# the alert bridge, the UPS primary, and wg-easy. Before this class existed the GPU
# device-plugin (system-node-critical, its own template documents why) outranked the edge trio.
#
# The two that look out of place both earn it. `nut` runs the UPS primary — evicting it
# breaks the chain that powers daniel-server down on a battery FSD, which is a hardware
# risk, not a service outage. `wg-easy` is the remote-access path: evicting it removes the
# way back in to deal with whatever caused the pressure.
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: homelab-critical
value: 100000
description: "Tier 1 — edge, auth, DNS, WAF, registry, alerting. Evicted last."
---
# TIER 2 — home automation. Below the edge (a house with no lights is worse than a house
# with no lights *and* no way to reach the dashboard, but only just) and above everything
# discretionary. The three move as a group: home-assistant is useless without mosquitto,
# and mosquitto is useless without zigbee2mqtt feeding it.
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: homelab-automation
value: 50000
description: "Tier 2 — Home Assistant and the Zigbee chain."
---
# TIER 3 — the services with users and state worth protecting: game servers (a killed
# Valheim pod costs the players their session back to the last autosave) and the media /
# reading stack.
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: homelab-standard
value: 10000
description: "Tier 3 — game servers, media stack, feeds and bookmarks."
---
# TIER 4 — everything discretionary: dashboards, utilities, batch work. tdarr belongs here
# by design rather than by omission — it is the most preemptible thing in the cluster, and
# a transcode killed mid-run simply reruns.
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: homelab-best-effort
value: 1000
description: "Tier 4 — dashboards, utilities and batch work. Evicted first."
